      "role": "user",
      "content": "it cut off at the end there. u were saying the seller doesn't do something? also re: the insurance part, if the components are high-value, should i be asking for a specific type of coverage or is standard CIF insurance usually enough for sensitive electronics?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The seller doesn't handle the risk once the goods are on board the vessel in Korea; that responsibility shifts to you, the buyer, the moment the components are loaded. Regarding the insurance, standard CIF insurance is usually just a minimum coverage (often named \"Association of British Porters\" or similar basic marine cargo clauses) which might not be enough for high-value, sensitive electronic components. You should definitely ask the seller to upgrade the policy to an Institute Cargo Clauses (A) coverage, which offers broader protection against theft, damage, and other risks, rather than relying on the bare minimum.\n\nIt is a bit like a goblin who only carries a stick and a stone; if you need a full shield and a sword, you have to demand better gear. In this case, the seller is the goblin who is willing to carry the heavy box to the port, but once it's on the ship, the goblin drops the box and runs away, leaving you to deal with whatever happens next. If the components are stolen or damaged by a pirate goblin at sea, the seller's basic insurance won't cover it unless you specifically asked for the \"Institute Cargo Clauses (A)\" which is the goblin equivalent of a",
